# Matcher specs

- Follow shared conventions in `spec.mdc` (named `subject`, `describe`/`context`, `described_class`).
- Boot via `rails_helper`; `MatcherHelpers` is auto-included for `spec/matchers`.
- Prefer `RSpec.describe ActiveStorageValidations::Matchers::SomeValidatorMatcher`.
- Use shared examples: `"checks if is a valid active storage attribute"`, `"checks if is valid"`, `"has custom matcher"`, `"has valid rspec message methods"`, `"works with both instance and class"`, `"works with allow_blank"`, `"works with custom message"`, `"works with context"`, `"works with except_on"`, `"works with timeout"`, `"base comparison validator matcher only match when exact value"`.
- Assertions go through helpers: `is_expected_to_match_for(klass)`, `is_expected_not_to_match_for(klass)`, failure/negated message helpers.
- Matcher `#description` / `#failure_message` / `#failure_message_when_negated` protect the public RSpec matcher API for gem consumers — keep those examples.
- Dummy matcher models live under `test/dummy/app/models/<validator>/matcher.rb`.
- Ship-facing matchers remain usable from Minitest/shoulda; do not require RSpec in production matcher code paths.
